  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <b>The feature for ELB access logging is not enabled by default; it requires explicit activation.</b> Elastic Load Balancer (ELB) access logging is an optional capability and must be manually enabled in order to begin capturing and storing request logs. If not enabled, the log files will remain empty, which explains why you are unable to find any records despite the application's activity.</p><p>Option <i>You lack the necessary permissions to view the access log files.</i> is incorrect because if permissions were the issue, you would likely see an error related to access or authorization, not an empty log.</p><p>Option <i>ELB access logs are retained for only seven days, after which they are permanently deleted.</i> is inaccurate; access logs are stored in your Amazon S3 bucket for as long as you wish. AWS does not enforce an automatic deletion after seven days. Retention is controlled by your S3 bucket's lifecycle policies.</p><p>Option <i>CloudWatch metrics must be enabled for access logs to be generated.</i> is incorrect because ELB access logs and CloudWatch metrics are independent features. Metrics do not have to be enabled for logging to occur.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> Always enable ELB access logging as soon as you deploy resources, so you don’t miss vital diagnostic and auditing data when issues arise later.</p>",
  "query": "<p>After setting up an application on EC2 instances behind an Elastic Load Balancer (ELB), customers report errors a few weeks after deployment. You attempt to review the ELB access logs for errors, but the logs are completely empty. Why might this be the case?</p>",
